---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

const file = `import { expandToNode } from 'langium/generate';
import { generateJavaFile } from 'langium-tools/lang/java';

generateJavaFile(
  'Door',
  'com.example.sm',
  gen,
  (imp) => expandToNode\`
    public class Door {
      private final \${imp('java.util.List')}<String> names;
      private final \${imp('java.awt.List')} peer;

      public Door(\${imp('java.util.List')}<String> names) {
        this.names = names;
      }
    }
  \`,
);`;

const emitted = `package com.example.sm;

import java.util.List;

public class Door {
  private final List<String> names;
  private final java.awt.List peer;

  public Door(List<String> names) {
    this.names = names;
  }
}`;

const direct = `import { JavaImportManager } from 'langium-tools/lang/java';

const imports = new JavaImportManager('com.example.sm');

imports.useClass('java.util.List');     // "List", and an import
imports.useClass('java.awt.List');       // "java.awt.List", no second import
imports.useClass('com.example.sm.Door'); // "Door", same package, no import

imports.generateImports();
// import java.util.List;`;

const path = `import { packageToPath } from 'langium-tools/lang/java';

packageToPath('com.example.sm'); // "com/example/sm"`;
---
<Base
  title="Java generation — langium-tools"
  description="Generate Java source files with a package declaration and an import list that survives classes sharing a simple name."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="doc-hero container">
      <div class="kicker">langium-tools/lang/java</div>
      <h1>Java files</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The awkward part of generating Java is the import block: it has to be written
        before the class, but you only know which types the body used afterwards, and
        two classes can share a simple name. <span class="inline-code">generateJavaFile</span>
        does that bookkeeping and hands the file to a <a href="/generator/">GeneratorManager</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="container">
      <h2>One function for a whole file</h2>
      <p>
        The body is a Langium <span class="inline-code">CompositeGeneratorNode</span>.
        Call the function you are given with a fully qualified name every time the
        body mentions a class. It returns the text that should appear at that spot:
        the simple name, or the full name when the simple name is already taken.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code={file} filename="door.ts" />
      <p>The file written for package <span class="inline-code">com.example.sm</span> is:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={emitted} lang="java" filename="com/example/sm/Door.java" />
      <p>
        <span class="inline-code">java.util.List</span> was registered first, so it owns
        the name <span class="inline-code">List</span>. <span class="inline-code">java.awt.List</span>
        stays qualified and is not imported. The path is
        <span class="inline-code">com/example/sm/Door.java</span>, relative to the generator target.
        An optional last argument is the usual <span class="inline-code">CreateFileOptions</span>
        (<span class="inline-code">overwrite</span>, <span class="inline-code">target</span>).
      </p>

      <h2>Imports on their own</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="inline-code">JavaImportManager</span> is what
        <span class="inline-code">generateJavaFile</span> uses. Construct it with the
        package of the file being generated. Classes in that package are not imported.
        <span class="inline-code">generateImports</span> returns the import statements,
        sorted, separated by the platform newline. Call <span class="inline-code">useClass</span>
        while building the body, and <span class="inline-code">generateImports</span> after
        the body is done. The import block is empty when nothing outside the current
        package was used.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code={direct} filename="imports.ts" />

      <h2>Package to directory</h2>
      <CodeBlock code={path} filename="path.ts" />

      <div class="note">
        <p>
          <span class="inline-code">java.lang</span> is not special-cased yet. Passing
          <span class="inline-code">java.lang.String</span> produces an import. Prefer to
          write <span class="inline-code">String</span> in the body and not register it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        API: <a href={`${SITE.typedoc}/functions/lang.java.generateJavaFile.html`}>generateJavaFile</a>,
        <a href={`${SITE.typedoc}/classes/lang.java.JavaImportManager.html`}>JavaImportManager</a>,
        <a href={`${SITE.typedoc}/functions/lang.java.packageToPath.html`}>packageToPath</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="next-guides">
        <a href="/generator/">← Code generation</a>
        <a href="/helpers/">Text helpers →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
